//
// THE JAMMER SWITCH — turning your own core off.
//
// BACKLOG C3.  The core IS the jammer (`SETTING.md` § The recruitment:
// *"we can provide you with a multi-frequency radio jammer.  That will
// prevent this AI from coordinating the individual robots"*), and until
// now it was a fact about the world rather than a thing the player
// touches.  This is the switch.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ What the jammer actually DOES, mechanically ──────────────
//
// Three things, and the fiction makes them one act:
//
//   ⚠ **it CUTS a robot off** — a robot that enters the bubble loses
//     its errand and is one of the accreting wave (`errand.loft` § The
//     bubble is the whole mechanic);
//   ⚠ **so it is why there are waves at all** — `SETTING.md` § Why
//     waves happen calls a wave *"the gradual accretion of individual
//     cut-off robots"*;
//   ⚠ **and it is why there is anything to salvage** — the pay is
//     *"any interesting materials that the AI used to build these
//     robots"*, which arrive as bodies in front of a wall.
//
// So the switch has a downside on BOTH sides, which is what
// `DESIGN.md` § What kind of game this is asks of a mechanic: *does
// this put something in the player's hands at a moment when using it
// costs them something?*  Leave it on and the waves keep coming; turn
// it off and your only income stops.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ It is NOT a panic button, and that is the whole balance ──
//
// **Turning it off stops the SUPPLY and never the SIEGE.**  Losing an
// errand is one way (`@X276`), so every robot already cut off is still
// cut off, still walking to the core, still chewing the wall it stopped
// at.  A player who hits the switch with a wave on the perimeter has
// bought nothing for this wave and given up the salvage they were about
// to collect from it.
//
// ⚠ That is what keeps `ROADMAP.md`'s item 4, the SCRAMBLE, the answer
// to being overrun.  A switch that made the wave walk away would have
// retired the mechanic the game is named after.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ Clearing still CLEARS; it just pays nothing ──────────────
//
// The salvage stops as INCOME, never as work.  A player must still be
// able to open a kill zone that bodies are ramping shut
// (`ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Bodies are terrain) — taking that away would
// make the switch a trap rather than a decision.  So `jammer_loot_rate`
// answers 0.0 and `height_clear` is untouched: you can tidy up with the
// core off, you are just working for free.
//
// ⚠ Which is also why toggling cannot be farmed.  Both effects are
// CONTINUOUS: switching on for one tick to bank a heap earns one tick's
// worth, and clearing a heap takes many.  To be paid you have to leave
// it on while you work, which is exactly the decision.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ The field stores OFF, and that is [loft#914] ─────────────
//
// A struct literal that omits a field takes that field's default
// SILENTLY, and a `boolean` defaults to `false`.  Store *on* and every
// `WaveState { … }` written anywhere in the repo would land with the
// core DARK — no waves, no income, and a suite full of scenarios
// measuring a game nobody plays.  Store *off* and the silent default is
// the way the game already worked.
//
// Same argument `wallet.loft` § Points SPENT, not points left makes for
// its ledger and `errand.loft` makes for `Enemy.errand`: **the neutral
// value has to be today's game.**
//
// ── What this does NOT decide ───────────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠ **The end game.**  `DESIGN.md` § Shutting down the scrambler gives
// this switch a second job — the swarm becomes coordinated and turns on
// the ancient ones, and the same device becomes a LURE deciding where
// they fight.  None of that is here: tier 3 does not exist, so the only
// thing the off state can be priced against today is the player's own
// base, and that is what `tests/c3_the_jammer.loft` measures.
//
// ⚠ **Anything diegetic.**  `DESIGN.md` § Two surface signals gives the
// core a top colour and a bottom pulse; a dark core is what a player
// should SEE, and nothing here draws.

use wallet;

// One core's jamming, as a switch.
//
// ⚠ A struct rather than a bare boolean on `WaveState`, for the reason
// `Wallet` is one: the field name and its polarity are a trap (§ The
// field stores OFF), so the rule lives in one place with the doors that
// state it the right way round.
pub struct Jammer {
    off: boolean,
}

// Factory: a core doing its job.
//
// ⚠ Named `_new` and not `_empty`, the distinction `wallet_new` draws:
// an "empty jammer" is the opposite of what this returns.
pub fn jammer_new() -> Jammer {
    Jammer { off: false }
}

// Is the core jamming?
//
// ⚠⚠ **Every reader asks THIS and never the field.**  `off` is stored
// so the silent default lands on today's game; `on` is what the rules
// are written in, and mixing the two is a sign error nothing would
// catch — a suite where the waves never come is green.
//
// Example: @DRY-120, @DRY-122
pub fn jammer_on(j: const Jammer) -> boolean {
    !j.off
}

// Switch it on or off outright.
//
// ⚠ A SET rather than a toggle, and `script.loft`'s `paint on` / `paint
// off` gives the reason: a scenario says what it WANTS, so a reader can
// tell what a line does without replaying the file to find out which
// way the flag was pointing.  The TOGGLE below is the player's
// affordance and is a different verb on purpose.
pub fn jammer_set(j: Jammer, on: boolean) {
    j.off = !on;
}

// Flip it, and answer the state it is now in.
//
// ⚠ It answers the NEW state rather than the old one, because the one
// caller that matters is a key press and what a player wants to know is
// what they just did.
//
// Example: @DRY-121
pub fn jammer_toggle(j: Jammer) -> boolean {
    j.off = !j.off;
    !j.off
}

// What a metre of this rubble is worth, given the state of the core.
//
// ⚠⚠ **The ONE door the salvage income goes through** (`spawn.loft`'s
// two payment sites, the player's and the crew's).  Writing the guard
// at each of them instead would be two copies of one rule, and the way
// they drift is a crew that goes on being paid after the core went
// dark.
//
// ⚠ It wraps `wallet.loft::loot_rate` rather than replacing it: what a
// SOURCE is worth is still that file's table, and this only says
// whether anybody is paying today.
//
// Example: @DRY-123
pub fn jammer_loot_rate(j: const Jammer, source: integer) -> float {
    if !jammer_on(j) { return 0.0; }
    loot_rate(source)
}
